 lesson('p2','Making decisions',12,'An if statement runs a block of code when a condition is true. elif checks another condition; else handles the remaining case.\n\nPython uses indentation to group statements. Use four spaces consistently. == checks equality, while = assigns a value.\n\nTRY IT: Change score to 40, 65, and 90. Predict which message appears each time.', 'score = 75\nif score >= 80:\n    print("Excellent progress!")\nelif score >= 50:\n    print("Keep practising!")\nelse:\n    print("Try the lesson again.")', 'python'),
 lesson('p3','Loops and lists',15,'A list stores several values in order. A for loop can visit each item. range(3) produces 0, 1, and 2; the stopping number is not included.\n\nLoops reduce repeated code. A while loop continues while a condition is true. Make sure its condition can eventually become false to avoid an endless loop.\n\nTRY IT: Add a fourth subject to the list and predict how many lines will be printed.', 'subjects = ["Maths", "Science", "Coding"]\nfor subject in subjects:\n    print("I am learning", subject)\n\nfor number in range(3):\n    print(number)', 'python')
